---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { path } from "../lib/paths";
---

<Base
  title="YAATT"
  description="Yet Another API Test Tool. Write API tests as YAML files, run the Docker image, and see a clear pass or fail."
>
  <main>
    <section class="hero wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Yet Another API Test Tool</p>
      <h1>API testing shouldn’t feel like filing taxes.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Write a test as a YAML file. Run the Docker image. YAATT sends the
        requests, runs the assertions, and prints a pass or fail. No clone, no
        extra app, no special test language.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href={path("/docs/")}>Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/iamuzor/yaatt">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline" aria-label="How YAATT runs">
        <article>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>YAML in</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>Requests run</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>Assertions checked</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>Results out</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section wrap">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <h2>The job was always simple.</h2>
          <p>
            Call an API. Write assertions on the reply. Folders, extra syntax,
            and workspace sync get in the way of that.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="pain-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Postman is great for clicking buttons.</strong>
            The tests are hard to read, and harder to review in a pull request.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Special test languages look nice at first.</strong>
            You end up maintaining the framework instead of the tests.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>YAATT is the file you already wanted.</strong>
            Name each request. Say what the response should look like. That is the test.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section wrap">
      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <p>If you can write a YAML file, you can write a test.</p>
      <div class="features" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <article>
          <h3>YAML first</h3>
          <p>Write tests in YAML so they stay easy to scan. JSON works too. Put files in <code>tests/</code>.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Editor help</h3>
          <p>Add <code>schema.json</code> and your editor can complete fields and catch mistakes.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Just Docker</h3>
          <p>Pull <code>iamuzorr/yaatt</code> and mount <code>tests/</code>. You never have to clone this repo to run tests.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Assertions that read clearly</h3>
          <p>Equals, not empty, status code, and a few more. Each line says what it is asserting.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Reuse values</h3>
          <p>Take a field from an earlier response, read an environment variable, or generate a unique id.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Readable output</h3>
          <p>Every request and assertion is printed. The run passes or fails like any other test command.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section wrap">
      <h2>A test is just a file</h2>
      <p>
        YAATT sends each request from top to bottom. It remembers each response
        by the name you gave it, then runs your assertions against those
        replies.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
          <span>tests/catalog.yml</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-grid">
          <div class="terminal-pane">
            <div class="terminal-label">Suite</div>
            <pre>{`name: Product catalog
requests:
  get_products:
    method: get
    url: https://dummyjson.com/products
  add_product:
    method: post
    url: https://dummyjson.com/products/add
    body:
      title: hello_world
      price: "{{gen:number}}"
assertions:
  - property: get_products
    type: status_code
    value: 200
  - property: get_products.products
    type: is_not_empty`}</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="terminal-pane">
            <div class="terminal-label">Output</div>
            <pre><span class="dim">TEST SUITE</span>
Product catalog (catalog.yml)

<span class="dim">REQUESTS</span>
🚀 get_products    200    0.31 secs
🚀 add_product     201    0.42 secs

<span class="dim">ASSERTIONS</span>
<span class="pass">Pass  get_products status code is "200"</span>
<span class="pass">Pass  get_products.products is not empty</span>

<span class="pass">RESULT: PASSED</span> <span class="dim">(2 passed, 0.89s)</span></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section wrap">
      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <div class="start-grid" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <pre>{`mkdir -p tests
docker run --rm \\
  -v $(pwd)/tests:/app/tests \\
  -t iamuzorr/yaatt`}</pre>
        <p class="note">
          Put YAML in <code>tests/</code>, then run the published image. A
          Makefile, environment variables, and CI examples are in the
          <a href={path("/docs/")}>usage docs</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
